### Comprehensive Study of Set Implementations with Unique Word Counting

#### 1. Why `Set` Rejects Duplicates:
A `Set` models the mathematical set abstraction. When an element is added:
- In `HashSet` / `LinkedHashSet`: It computes `hashCode()`, locates the bucket, and checks `equals()`. If an identical element exists, the duplicate is rejected and `add()` returns `false`.
- In `TreeSet`: It invokes `compareTo()` or a custom `Comparator`. If `compareTo() == 0`, the duplicate is rejected.

#### 2. Detailed Technical Comparison Table:
| Comparison Parameter | `HashSet` | `LinkedHashSet` | `TreeSet` |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Sorting & Order** | None (pseudo-random bucket distribution). | Strictly preserves insertion sequence. | Strictly sorted ascending ($A \dots Z, 1 \dots n$). |
| **Search Time** | **$O(1)$** constant time. | **$O(1)$** constant time. | **$O(\log n)$** logarithmic time. |
| **Interface Implemented**| `Set` | `Set` | `SortedSet`, `NavigableSet` |
| **Internal Mechanism** | Backed by `HashMap`. | Backed by `LinkedHashMap`. | Backed by `TreeMap` (Red-Black Tree). |

#### 3. University Problem Solution: Count Unique Words from File/Text Using HashSet & TreeSet:
```java
import java.util.*;

public class UniqueWordCounter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String passage = "Java is an object oriented language Java is secure Java is robust and secure";

        String[] tokens = passage.toLowerCase().split("\\s+");

        // 1. HashSet for Fast Unique Extraction
        Set<String> uniqueWords = new HashSet<>(Arrays.asList(tokens));

        // 2. TreeSet for Alphabetically Sorted Unique Extraction
        Set<String> sortedUnique = new TreeSet<>(uniqueWords);

        System.out.println("Original Word Count: " + tokens.length);
        System.out.println("Unique Word Count:   " + uniqueWords.size());
        System.out.println("Unique Words (Unordered HashSet): " + uniqueWords);
        System.out.println("Unique Words (Sorted TreeSet):    " + sortedUnique);
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{HashSet provides optimal constant-time uniqueness filtering, while TreeSet guarantees natural sorted ordering.}$$
